 -3.88, "analyst_adjustment": 0.68, "key_factors": ["September polls from Democratic-leaning firms show a tied race, well inside the district's R+6 lean", "Salazar's ads breaking with Trump on immigration signal perceived Hispanic-voter vulnerability", "Incumbent holds a large cash advantage ($2.3M vs $325K) and all nine ratings favor Republicans", "Strong Democratic national environment with national Democrats stumping in Florida battlegrounds"], "rationale": "The district's Republican lean, Salazar's incumbency, and her dominant fundraising edge outweigh a favorable Democratic environment, but recent polling showing a dead heat and her defensive immigration messaging keep this well short of Likely R.", "watch": "Independent polling confirming the tied race, or a deepening Salazar-Trump rift among Miami-Dade Hispanic voters, could push this to a true tossup.", "confidence": "medium", "notes": ["Incumbent María Elvira Salazar (R) is running."]}, "overview": "Representative María Elvira Salazar seeks re-election in a Miami-Dade district that leans Republican by six points, though recent signs point to softening. Challenger Eliott Rodriguez has raised a credible sum but trails badly in cash. Two September surveys from Democratic-leaning pollsters show the race essentially tied, a sharper picture than the district's fundamentals suggest, and national Democrats have begun stumping in Florida's battleground seats.\n\nSalazar is running ads criticizing the administration's immigration enforcement, arguing Hispanic voters feel betrayed — a sign she perceives vulnerability among the coalition that powered her rise, though it may also help her distance herself from unpopular policies. Rating outlets uniformly favor Republicans, split between Lean R and Likely R, and prediction markets give Democrats roughly a one-in-three chance. Her incumbency and financial edge keep her favored, but the expected margin is unusually narrow for a seated incumbent.", "history": [{"date": "202